Layer 4 (Transport Layer) operates at the IP/TCP/UDP layer, routing packets without inspecting the application payload.

Layer 7 (Application Layer) operates at the application layer (HTTP/HTTPS, gRPC), terminating client connections to evaluate headers, cookies, and request paths before routing.

Routing Algorithms include Round-Robin / Weighted, Least Connections, and Consistent Hashing.

### Reverse Proxies & Edge Gateways

Reverse proxies act as administrative gateways in front of backend applications, handling non-business infrastructure concerns such as SSL/TLS Termination, Rate Limiting & Throttling, and Request Routing & Rewriting.

## 3. Data Layer Architecture, Caching & Replication

### Caching Strategies & Hazards

```
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant Client
    participant Cache as Redis Cache
    participant DB as Relational Database

    Note over Client, DB: Cache-Aside Pattern
    Client->>Cache: Read Key
    alt Cache Hit
        Cache-->>Client: Return Cached Data
    else Cache Miss
        Cache-->>Client: Null / Miss
        Client->>DB: Query Primary Record
        DB-->>Client: Return Data
        Client->>Cache: Populate Key with TTL
    end

```

#### Cache Strategies

1. Cache-Aside (Passive): Application handles read misses and writes back to cache. Minimizes unused cache memory.
2. Write-Through: Synchronously writes to cache and underlying store simultaneously.
3. Write-Back (Write-Behind): Updates cache immediately; writes to database asynchronously in batches.

#### Cache Hazards & Mitigations

Cache Stampede: Concurrency surge on a cache miss overwhelming the DB. Mitigation involves single-flight locks or probabilistic early expiration.

Cache Penetration: Querying non-existent keys repeatedly. Mitigation involves storing null objects or using Bloom Filters.

Cache Avalanche: Mass key expiration at the same time. Mitigation involves adding randomized jitter to TTLs.

## 4. Microservices & Distributed System Boundaries

### API Gateways & Service Meshes

```
graph TD
    Client[External Client] -->|HTTPS / OAuth2 Token| GW[API Gateway Ingress]
    
    subgraph Service Mesh Domain
        GW -->|mTLS| MeshA[Service A - Envoy Sidecar]
        MeshA <-->|mTLS / gRPC| MeshB[Service B - Envoy Sidecar]
    end

    ControlPlane[Service Mesh Control Plane] -.-> MeshA
    ControlPlane -.-> MeshB

```

API Gateway (North-South Traffic) manages ingress traffic from external clients entering the internal system network, handling authentication validation, rate limiting, and routing.

Service Mesh (East-West Traffic) manages communication between internal microservices using proxy sidecars, handling service discovery, circuit breaking, mTLS encryption, and automatic retries.

