### End-to-End E-Commerce System Blueprint

```
graph TD
    Users[Global Users] --> GeoDNS[GeoDNS / Anycast Routing]
    
    subgraph Primary Region: Active
        GeoDNS --> CDN1[Edge CDN & WAF]
        CDN1 --> LB1[L4 / L7 Load Balancers]
        LB1 --> GW1[API Gateway]
        GW1 --> SvcRead1[Catalog Read Service]
        GW1 --> SvcWrite1[Stateful Order Engine]
        SvcRead1 --> Cache1[(Redis Cluster)]
        SvcWrite1 --> Kafka1[[Apache Kafka Event Bus]]
        Kafka1 --> Workers1[Async Inventory & Billing Workers]
        SvcWrite1 --> DB1[(Primary DB - Sharded Write)]
    end

    subgraph Secondary Region: Passive Standby
        GeoDNS -.-> CDN2[Edge CDN & WAF]
        CDN2 -.-> LB2[L4 / L7 Load Balancers]
        LB2 -.-> GW2[API Gateway]
        GW2 -.-> SvcRead2[Catalog Read Service]
        SvcRead2 -.-> Cache2[(Redis Cluster)]
        DB2[(Secondary DB - Read Replica)]
    end

    DB1 == Sync / Async Replication ==> DB2

```

#### Walkthrough

1. Traffic Ingestion: GeoDNS routes users to the nearest POP. The WAF drops malicious traffic at the edge.
2. Ingress & Auth: API Gateway validates JWT signatures, enforces rate limits, and routes clean requests via gRPC.
3. Optimized Reads: Product searches hit a Cache-Aside Redis Cluster with mutex locks to handle heavy flash-sale concurrency.
4. Order Execution & Event Bus: Writes commit to a primary sharded database. The Order Service publishes an OrderPlaced event to Apache Kafka and returns an HTTP 202 Accepted response immediately.
5. Decoupled Consumers: Asynchronous worker services process payment charging, stock management, and email notifications independently via Kafka consumer groups.

