Layer 4 (Transport Layer)
Operates at the IP/TCP/UDP layer, routing packets without inspecting the application payload. Decisions are made using source/destination IP addresses and ports.

Performance: High throughput with minimal CPU overhead (line-rate processing).
Trade-off: Lacks application visibility; cannot inspect HTTP headers, cookies, or request paths.
Layer 7 (Application Layer)
Operates at the application layer (HTTP/HTTPS, gRPC), terminating client connections to evaluate headers, cookies, content types, and request paths before routing.

Performance: Higher CPU overhead due to TLS termination and deep packet inspection.
Trade-off: Enables content-based routing, URL rewriting, rate limiting, and context-aware security.
Routing Algorithms
Round-Robin / Weighted Round-Robin
Distributes requests sequentially across a list of servers. Weighted variants route proportionately higher volumes to hosts with larger compute capacities.
Least Connections / Weighted Least Connections
Directs incoming traffic to the instance with the fewest active connections. This prevents slow requests from overloading single instances.
IP Hash / Consistent Hashing
Hashes client source parameters (e.g., IP address) to route requests to the same server node. Consistent hashing minimizes key redistribution when scaling cluster membership.
Reverse Proxies & Edge Gateways
Reverse proxies act as administrative gateways in front of backend applications, handling infrastructure concerns outside the core business logic.

SSL/TLS Termination: Offloads CPU-intensive cryptographic operations from backend services. Traffic between the proxy and internal networks can run over managed, unencrypted VPC connections or dedicated internal TLS sessions.
Rate Limiting & Throttling: Protects downstream applications from denial-of-service attempts using token bucket or leaky bucket algorithms to enforce request quotas.
Request Routing & Rewriting: Standardizes external paths to internal endpoints, injects tracing headers (e.g., X-Request-ID), and handles cross-origin policies (CORS).
Edge Caching & Content Delivery Networks (CDNs)
CDNs place static assets and cached dynamic responses near end users, reducing latency and offloading origin traffic.

Cache-Control Directives: Uses standard HTTP headers (Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, s-maxage=86400, ETag, Last-Modified) to define validation and staleness constraints across distributed caches.
Origin Shielding: Introduces a centralized proxy tier between edge locations and origin servers to aggregate cache-miss traffic and mitigate origin server spikes.
Edge Compute: Runs lightweight code (e.g., WebAssembly, edge workers) directly at edge nodes to personalize content, rewrite URLs, and enforce auth rules closer to the user.
