Relational (SQL)
Structured models bound by rigid schemas, enforcing ACID properties (Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability) through relational joins and multi-version concurrency control (MVCC).

Ideal for: Financial ledgers, transaction management, and complex relational models.
Non-Relational (NoSQL)
Optimized for horizontal scaling and variable schemas, prioritizing performance and availability (BASE: Basically Available, Soft-state, Eventual consistency).

Key-Value Stores: High-throughput, low-latency lookups by primary key (e.g., Redis, DynamoDB).
Document Databases: Hierarchical semi-structured storage, often using JSON or BSON formats (e.g., MongoDB).
Wide-Column Stores: Optimized for sequential writes and large-scale analytical aggregation (e.g., Apache Cassandra).
Graph Databases: Optimized for index-free adjacency traversals in highly interconnected domain models (e.g., Neo4j).
Database Scaling
Vertical vs. Horizontal Scaling
Vertical (Scale-Up): Increases memory, compute, and IOPS on a single node. Limited by hardware capacity and cost curves.
Horizontal (Scale-Out): Adds independent database nodes across a network, requiring distributed consensus algorithms and data partitioning strategies.
Read Replicas & Replication Models
Synchronous Replication: Primary nodes write changes to replica logs before confirming transactions to clients. Guarantees read consistency, but increases write latency and depends on node availability.
Asynchronous Replication: Primary nodes confirm transactions immediately and replicate changes asynchronously. Provides low write latency, but introduces replication lag and the potential for dirty reads or data loss during failovers.
Sharding & Partitioning Mechanics
Partitions large datasets into sub-tables across multiple database nodes.

Range-Based Partitioning: Divides data using explicit key ranges. Simple to set up, but susceptible to hot spots when accessing sequential data.
Hash-Based Partitioning: Applies cryptographic or consistent hashing functions to a shard key to evenly distribute writes across database nodes.
Directory-Based Partitioning: Uses a centralized lookup service to map shard keys to physical instances, allowing dynamic partition rebalancing.

