Point-to-Point (Competing Consumers / AMQP)
Messages are delivered to a queue, and each message is processed by a single consumer worker (e.g., RabbitMQ).

Ideal for: Task distribution, background processing, and command execution pipelines.
Publish-Subscribe (Distributed Event Log)
Events are written to persistent, append-only topic logs (e.g., Apache Kafka). Multiple consumers track their own read offsets to process event streams independently at their own pace.

Ideal for: Event-sourcing, analytics pipelines, stream processing, and multi-consumer notifications.
Dead Letter Queues (DLQ)
Messages that fail processing due to malformed payloads or unhandled business errors are automatically routed to a isolated Dead Letter Queue after a maximum retry threshold. This isolates unprocessable messages for asynchronous debugging without blocking queue throughput.
Real-World Enterprise Scenario & Detailed Analysis
Context
An enterprise ride-sharing platform handles real-time vehicle positioning, fare calculation, and post-ride billing.
Architectural Breakdown
To maintain operational isolation and system reliability, the architecture decouples real-time tracking from billing flows:

Ingress and Internal Mesh: Driver location updates hit an API Gateway, which routes them directly to a Location Tracking Microservice. Communication between services relies on an Envoy-based Service Mesh with automated circuit breaking. If the Fare Computation service slows down, its circuit opens, allowing the system to serve estimated fallback metrics without crashing dependent endpoints.
Asynchronous Message Backbone: When a ride finishes, the Ride Management Service writes a RideCompleted event to an Apache Kafka topic.
Decoupled Consumer Processing:
The Billing Service reads the message to charge the customer's credit card.
The Driver Pay Service reads the same message from its own consumer group offset to calculate earnings.
The Analytics Engine reads the stream asynchronously to update regional dynamic pricing models.
Fault Tolerance: If the Billing Service experiences downstream network issues, unprocessable billing events pass through 3 retry attempts before routing to a dedicated billing-dlq. The core system continues operating normally, and operators can replay failed messages from the DLQ once issues are resolved.


