Active-Passive Topologies
Traffic routes to a single primary datacenter while a secondary passive datacenter remains on standby.

Replication: Databases replicate state asynchronously or synchronously to the standby region.
Failover: During an outage, global DNS or Anycast layers switch traffic to the standby region.
Trade-off: Lower resource cost during normal operations, but introduces recovery time (RTO) and potential data loss (RPO).
Active-Active Multi-Region Topologies
Traffic distributes concurrently across multiple geographic regions running active application stacks.

Replication: Requires multi-region database setups or localized data sharding to prevent write conflicts across regions.
Failover: Provides immediate failure recovery without manual failover steps.
Trade-off: Higher operational cost and increased architectural complexity to manage data consistency.
Key Metrics
Recovery Point Objective (RPO): The maximum acceptable age of un-replicated data lost during a system failure.
Recovery Time Objective (RTO): The maximum acceptable downtime required to restore normal system operations after an outage.
